1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,941
Can you wear your cycling helmet for skating?

2
00:00:02,941 --> 00:00:10,198
By the end of this guide, you will know when a bike helmet is enough, and when to buy a skating helmet instead.

3
00:00:10,198 --> 00:00:13,336
This is one of the most common questions we get.

4
00:00:13,336 --> 00:00:17,389
Usually it comes from a parent who already owns a bike helmet.

5
00:00:17,389 --> 00:00:19,024
The short answer is this.

6
00:00:19,024 --> 00:00:23,338
A certified cycling helmet is almost always better than no helmet.

7
00:00:23,338 --> 00:00:26,738
And in Europe, the same standard covers both sports.

8
00:00:26,738 --> 00:00:28,764
But there are real differences.

9
00:00:28,764 --> 00:00:31,379
One of them matters a lot for beginners.

10
00:00:32,079 --> 00:00:34,389
Start with the certification.

11
00:00:34,389 --> 00:00:37,893
EN1078 is the European standard for helmets.

12
00:00:37,893 --> 00:00:39,406
Its scope is clear.

13
00:00:39,406 --> 00:00:45,696
It covers helmets for cyclists, and for users of skateboards and roller skates.

14
00:00:45,696 --> 00:00:48,165
One standard, three activities.

15
00:00:48,165 --> 00:00:53,421
So a helmet with a CE EN1078 mark has been tested for skating use.

16
00:00:53,421 --> 00:00:58,278
That is true whether the box shows a bicycle or a skateboard.

17
00:00:58,978 --> 00:01:01,395
This is the part most people miss.

18
00:01:01,395 --> 00:01:05,304
The question is not really bike helmet or skate helmet.

19
00:01:05,304 --> 00:01:11,558
The question is whether the helmet in your hands has been tested to any standard at all.

20
00:01:11,558 --> 00:01:16,817
A great many cheap helmets sold for skating carry no stated certification.

21
00:01:16,817 --> 00:01:21,935
So an EN1078 cycling helmet from a reputable brand is the better choice.

22
00:01:21,935 --> 00:01:26,981
It beats an uncertified helmet that is simply sold as a skating helmet.

23
00:01:27,681 --> 00:01:30,546
Now, where does a cycling helmet fall short?

24
00:01:30,546 --> 00:01:33,021
There are three practical differences.

25
00:01:33,021 --> 00:01:35,040
The first is the important one.

26
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:37,189
Coverage at the back of the head.

27
00:01:37,189 --> 00:01:41,813
Road cycling helmets are shaped around the risk of going over the bars.

28
00:01:41,813 --> 00:01:45,069
So they extend forward and sit higher at the back.

29
00:01:45,069 --> 00:01:49,302
Skate style helmets come further down over the back of the skull.

30
00:01:49,302 --> 00:01:50,930
Here is why that matters.

31
00:01:50,930 --> 00:01:53,014
Beginner skaters fall backwards.

32
00:01:53,014 --> 00:01:55,359
Your feet slide out in front of you.

33
00:01:55,359 --> 00:01:59,331
The back of your head is the first thing to reach the ground.

34
00:01:59,331 --> 00:02:02,718
That is exactly the area a road helmet covers least.

35
00:02:02,718 --> 00:02:09,296
If you are learning, or buying for a child who is learning, that extra rear coverage is not a detail.

36
00:02:09,995 --> 00:02:13,203
The second difference is repeated impacts.

37
00:02:13,203 --> 00:02:17,862
Most EN1078 helmets, cycling and skating alike, use EPS foam.

38
00:02:17,862 --> 00:02:22,827
That foam crushes once to absorb energy, and it does not recover.

39
00:02:22,827 --> 00:02:25,423
So they are single impact helmets.

40
00:02:25,423 --> 00:02:32,450
Some skate style helmets are built to a different standard, such as the American ASTM F1492.

41
00:02:32,450 --> 00:02:34,741
It tests for multiple impacts.

42
00:02:34,741 --> 00:02:40,317
That is useful if you are learning tricks and going down again and again.

43
00:02:40,317 --> 00:02:42,455
Either way, follow one rule.

44
00:02:42,455 --> 00:02:48,489
Replace any helmet after a significant impact, even if the shell looks perfect.

45
00:02:48,489 --> 00:02:52,307
The damage is in the liner, and you cannot see it.

46
00:02:53,007 --> 00:02:56,700
The third difference is ventilation, weight and shape.

47
00:02:56,700 --> 00:03:00,735
Road cycling helmets are usually well ventilated and light.

48
00:03:00,735 --> 00:03:03,607
That is why they transfer well to skating.

49
00:03:03,607 --> 00:03:07,710
Classic skate style helmets have a hard shell and few vents.

50
00:03:07,710 --> 00:03:08,598
They are hot.

51
00:03:08,598 --> 00:03:13,590
And in Indian conditions, a hot helmet is a helmet that stays in the bag.

52
00:03:13,590 --> 00:03:16,667
The best helmet is the one you actually wear.

53
00:03:17,367 --> 00:03:19,034
What about speed skating?

54
00:03:19,034 --> 00:03:24,698
For inline speed and road skating, a light, well ventilated helmet is the right tool.

55
00:03:24,698 --> 00:03:28,497
The needs look much more like cycling than skateboarding.

56
00:03:28,497 --> 00:03:30,696
You move fast in a straight line.

57
00:03:30,696 --> 00:03:31,829
You want airflow.

58
00:03:31,829 --> 00:03:36,094
And you want the lowest weight on your neck over a long session.

59
00:03:36,094 --> 00:03:43,091
That is why LiveLica skating helmets are shaped more like a road cycling helmet than a skate park helmet.

60
00:03:43,091 --> 00:03:45,757
Short track ice racing is the exception.

61
00:03:45,757 --> 00:03:50,955
Those helmets are a compact, low profile shell, made to sit under a race hood.

62
00:03:50,955 --> 00:03:54,221
They are designed with close pack racing in mind.

63
00:03:54,221 --> 00:03:57,086
A cycling helmet is not a substitute there.

64
00:03:57,786 --> 00:04:00,164
So should you buy a skating helmet?

65
00:04:00,164 --> 00:04:02,677
Be honest about which skater you are.

66
00:04:02,677 --> 00:04:09,742
If you are learning to skate, or buying for a child, get a skate style helmet with proper rear coverage.

67
00:04:09,742 --> 00:04:12,187
Backward falls are the whole reason.

68
00:04:12,187 --> 00:04:17,146
If you skate for fun and already own a certified cycling helmet, wear it.

69
00:04:17,146 --> 00:04:21,154
It is tested to the right standard, and it is on your head.

70
00:04:21,154 --> 00:04:22,988
That is most of the battle.

71
00:04:22,988 --> 00:04:28,830
For inline speed or road skating, put weight, ventilation and a certified shell first.

72
00:04:28,830 --> 00:04:31,072
A good road cycling helmet works.

73
00:04:31,072 --> 00:04:37,322
A purpose built skating helmet does the same job, with a fit made for your skating position.

74
00:04:37,322 --> 00:04:40,854
And for short track ice, buy the short track helmet.

75
00:04:41,554 --> 00:04:43,690
Whatever you choose, fit matters.

76
00:04:43,690 --> 00:04:47,379
A helmet that fits badly does only a fraction of its job.

77
00:04:47,379 --> 00:04:51,780
Measure around your head, about two centimetres above your eyebrows.

78
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:53,010
Buy to that number.

79
00:04:53,010 --> 00:04:57,734
The helmet should sit level, about two finger widths above your eyebrows.

80
00:04:57,734 --> 00:05:00,647
It should feel snug, with no pressure points.

81
00:05:00,647 --> 00:05:03,495
Shake your head before you tighten anything.

82
00:05:03,495 --> 00:05:05,307
The helmet should not shift.

83
00:05:05,307 --> 00:05:10,161
Then set the chin strap so two fingers fit between the strap and your chin.

84
00:05:10,861 --> 00:05:12,181
Here is the summary.

85
00:05:12,181 --> 00:05:15,615
A certified cycling helmet is a real skating helmet.

86
00:05:15,615 --> 00:05:17,001
The standard says so.

87
00:05:17,001 --> 00:05:23,141
It is weaker at the back of the head, and that matters most for the people who fall the most.

88
00:05:23,141 --> 00:05:25,650
If you already own one, wear it today.

89
00:05:25,650 --> 00:05:29,612
If you are buying for a beginner, buy for the backward fall.

90
00:05:29,612 --> 00:05:34,564
The full guide is on the LiveLica blog, and the link is in the description.
